---
title: stdlib.template — Named String Templates
author: LATERALUS contributors
abstract: Fill named <<name>> placeholders and choose how missing values are handled.
---

# stdlib.template

`stdlib.template` uses `<<name>>` as a named placeholder. Pass a map to
`render` and each matching placeholder is replaced with its value.

```lateralus
import stdlib.template

let values = {"name": "Ada", "language": "Lateralus"}
let greeting = stdlib.template.render(
    "Hello, <<name>>! Welcome to <<language>>.", values)
println(greeting)
```

The fixed output is:

```text
Hello, Ada! Welcome to Lateralus.
```

## Missing values

`render` preserves an unknown placeholder. This is useful when a template is
filled in stages and a later pass will provide the remaining value.

```lateralus
let values = {"name": "Ada"}
println(stdlib.template.render("Hello, <<name>>! Role: <<role>>.", values))
```

Output:

```text
Hello, Ada! Role: <<role>>.
```

When a missing value should be replaced immediately, use `render_default`:

```lateralus
println(stdlib.template.render_default(
    "Hello, <<name>>! Role: <<role>>.", values, "guest"))
```

Output:

```text
Hello, Ada! Role: guest.
```

The complete runnable example is [`examples/template_demo.ltl`](../../examples/template_demo.ltl).
Run it with `lateralus run examples/template_demo.ltl`.

